# The midnight pools (playtest 2, X13): what the six open places hold after curfew, and who is about. Most of the
# castle is sealed; what is open feels shady, and nights are riskier than days: a risky outcome queues
# `night_suspicion` at its own odds (M41). Illicit money (the ring, the high table, the stores, Grisk's parcels,
# stolen pages) is a third way to pay and pulls alignment toward the Hollow; hosting upstairs is legal, only risky. Harmless nights exist too.

# ---------------- The Gilded Newt, upstairs ----------------

@event newt_company
title: Company for the Evening
place: gilded_newt
slot: midnight
class: routine
tone: tense
# The first offer (feedback 2026-10-16): first on every Newt night (`@place always:`) until you take it; turning it
# down rests it for a few months. Once you are a regular, `newt_company_regular` takes its place.
when: not flag(newt_regular) and not (chosen(newt_company, right) and since(newt_company) < 3)
weight: 10
art: cup
tags: money danger
scene: An upstairs room at a tavern after hours, card tables under low lamps, well-dressed guests laughing, a student in borrowed finery refilling a glass.
text: The woman who runs the upstairs room looks you over. "The guests like good company. Laugh at the jokes, keep the glasses full, lose gracefully at cards. Nothing more: guests who want more are shown the stair. Every night is a different party, and every party pays."
> left: Take a seat upstairs
  hint: Paid company, a different guest each night.
  flag newt_regular
  find lead_newt_upstairs
  queue any in company_night now
  queue company_late_hour now
  if bonds(partner) > 0:
    queue night_partner_asks chance unlikely
  say: She hands you a borrowed jacket that almost fits. "Smile with your eyes," she says. "The guests can tell."
> right: Not tonight
  say: "Another night, then," says the woman, writing something in a small book. You wonder what.

@event newt_company_regular
title: The Upstairs Room
place: gilded_newt
slot: midnight
class: routine
tone: tense
# The regular's offer (feedback 2026-10-16): first on every Newt night once you've taken the work; a refusal rests it.
when: flag(newt_regular) and not (chosen(newt_company_regular, right) and since(newt_company_regular) < 3)
weight: 10
art: cup
tags: money danger
scene: An upstairs room at a tavern after hours, a landlady holding out a borrowed jacket to a student at the top of the stair.
text: {The woman who runs the upstairs room knows your face now. "There's a party tonight," she says, already holding out the jacket.|The landlady has your jacket on a peg with your name chalked above it. "Your table's waiting," she says.|"Your regulars asked after you," says the landlady, which is either flattering or a warning. Upstairs the lamps are lit.}
> left: Work the room
  hint: Paid company; tonight's guests decide the rest.
  queue any in company_night now
  queue company_late_hour now
  if bonds(partner) > 0:
    queue night_partner_asks chance unlikely
  say: {You button the jacket and go up. It fits a little better than last time.|You take your jacket from its peg and climb the stair into the lamplight.|Your regulars wave you over before you've reached the top of the stair.}
> right: Not tonight
  say: {"Next week, then," she says, and the jacket goes back on its peg.|She shrugs and chalks a line through tonight beside your name.|"They'll miss you," she says, and it sounds like the truth and the bill at once.}

@event newt_high_table
title: The High Table
place: gilded_newt
slot: midnight
type: menu
class: routine
tone: tense
# Every Newt night ends here, after the company (feedback 2026-10-16: `@place always:`, no cooldown).
when: not flag(grisk_gone)
weight: 9
art: cards
tags: money danger
scene: A round table in a back room under a single green lamp, piles of coins, a goblin in a velvet waistcoat keeping the bank.
text: {Behind the upstairs room there's a back room, and in it the high table. Grisk keeps the bank. Nobody here plays for crowns alone.|The high table again. Grisk nods to you as if you were a regular. You are becoming one.|Grisk has kept your seat warm, he says. It is not a comforting thought.} {A first-year's familiar sits in a cage beside the pot.|Someone has staked a silver wand and is sweating about it.|Somebody's signed promise is in the pot, folded small.}
> purse: Stake your purse
  hint: Guile against the table.
  meet grisk
  alignment -tiny
  gold -medium
  challenge deceive adept unlikely wild:
    win:
      gold +large
      gold +medium
      mood +small
      queue newt_high_table_raise now
      say: {You read the table like a book you've already finished. The pot slides over, and Grisk applauds politely with two fingers.|Your nerve holds and theirs doesn't. You walk home heavy with silver and hollow with nerves.|Somebody's folded promise is in the pot. You win it, and give it back to the owner unread. Grisk thinks that's hilarious.}
    lose:
      queue night_suspicion chance even
      say: {The cards turn on you, then the room does. You leave lighter, and a prefect's lamp is going round the courtyard.|You lose the pot and your temper with it. Grisk sympathises, with interest.|You lose, and Grisk offers to take a signed promise instead of the coin you're short. You pay in coin, all of it.}
> favour: Stake a favour to Grisk
  hint: Win big, or owe him a job.
  when: not flag(owes_grisk)
  meet grisk
  alignment -small
  challenge deceive adept- unlikely wild:
    win:
      gold +large
      say: You stake "one favour, to be named" and win. Grisk smiles, tears up the promise, and keeps the pieces. "Next time," he says.
    lose:
      flag owes_grisk
      mood -small
      queue grisk_names_favour in 1
      say: You lose, and Grisk folds your promise into his waistcoat pocket like a love letter. "I'll be in touch," he says. "Soon."
> health: Play the bone-dice
  hint: The old northern game. Win or bleed.
  alignment -tiny
  challenge feat adept- wild:
    win:
      gold +medium
      renown +tiny
      say: The bone-dice game is played with a knife between the fingers. You keep all ten and win the pot. Grisk calls you "dear heart" for the rest of the night.
    lose:
      health -medium
      gold +small
      say: You win the pot and lose a fair amount of skin from your left hand. Amaranth, in the morning, asks no questions, very loudly.
> leave: Watch one hand, then go
  say: {You watch a student lose a familiar on a bluff, and go home and hug yours, or your pillow.|You watch, learn and leave. Grisk raises his glass to your back.|You watch a signed promise change hands across the table, and decide never to sign anything in this room.}

@event night_partner_asks
title: Where Were You?
trigger: queued
defer: off_campus
class: incident
tone: tense
art: heart
tags: romance
cast:
  partner: any where bond(partner) == "partner" and around(partner)
scene: Two students on a cold stone stair in the morning, one holding a tavern's silver token found in the other's coat.
text: {partner} is waiting on the stair with a silver token from the Gilded Newt in {their} palm. It fell out of your coat. "The upstairs room," {they} say{s}. "People talk. I'd rather hear it from you."
> left: Tell {them} everything
  spark partner -tiny
  rel partner +small
  mood -small
  say: You tell the truth: the glasses, the cards, the guests, the stair. {partner} is quiet a long time. "Thank you for telling me," {they} say{s} at last, and means it, and it still costs you both.
> right: "It's nothing. A card game."
  spark partner -small
  alignment -tiny
  say: {partner} looks at the token, then at you, and puts it in your hand. "All right," {they} say{s}. It isn't. For a week {they} sit{s} a little further away at supper.

@event grisk_names_favour
title: The Favour, Named
trigger: queued
defer: off_campus
when: not flag(grisk_gone)
class: incident
tone: tense
actor: grisk
art: chain
tags: money danger
scene: A goblin in a velvet waistcoat waiting in a library alcove, a folded promise held up between two fingers.
text: Grisk is waiting in the library alcove with your promise between two fingers. "The favour," he says. "A small one. A crate goes from the boathouse to the village. Tonight. Or, if you prefer, the price of the crate." He smiles. "I always offer a choice."
> left: Move the crate
  unflag owes_grisk
  flag grisk_contact
  find lead_grisk_jetty
  alignment -small
  challenge deceive vs bram.guile +1:
    win:
      say: The crate clinks and, faintly, sings. You don't look inside. Grisk tears up your promise. "You're good at this," he says, which is the worst thing he could say.
    lose:
      gain watched
      mood -small
      say: Bram stops you on the jetty and lifts the tarp. Charm-wine. The crate is gone, the favour counts as paid, and Bram has your name.
> right: Pay him off
  unflag owes_grisk
  gold -large
  mood -small
  say: You count out the crowns and Grisk gives you back the promise with a small bow. It feels like buying your own name back. It is, more or less.

# ---------------- Company nights (feedback 2026-10-16) ----------------
# The pool behind `queue any tagged company_night`: each night upstairs is one party, drawn by the usual score. The
# pay is better than any shift, and so is the price: late nights, guests who push, and the castle finding out.

@thread company_night
from: scene

@event company_birthday
title: A Merchant's Birthday
thread: company_night
class: routine
tone: light
repeat: 3
art: cup
tags: money company_night
scene: A loud birthday party in a tavern's upstairs room, a stout merchant in a paper crown, a cake shaped like a ship.
text: {A cloth merchant from the capital is sixty tonight and wants a room full of young people laughing at his stories.|The cloth merchant is back with another party. He says it's his birthday again, and nobody dares to count.|Another of the merchant's birthdays. He has started inviting his rivals, so that they can watch him be adored.} The cake is shaped like one of his ships.
> left: Be the life of the party
  hint: Charm the room; big tips, a long night.
  challenge perform journeyman+:
    win:
      gold +large
      health -small
      say: {You laugh, sing, and dance with his aunt. He tips like a man buying a ship. You get home with the dawn and a sore throat.|He tells the story about the storm off the southern coast again, and you gasp in the right place. The tip is heavy; so are your feet.|You toast him in front of his rivals and he nearly cries. The purse he presses on you is shaped, faintly, like a ship.}
    lose:
      gold +small
      mood -small
      queue night_suspicion chance unlikely
      say: {You laugh a moment too late at the wrong story, and he notices. A thin tip, and a long walk home past the castle's lit windows.|You call his ship by his rival's ship's name. The room goes quiet. The landlady pays you off early.|His rivals are funnier than he is, and you show it. He doesn't forgive it, and the tip says so.}
> right: Keep the glasses full
  hint: A quiet night's pay, on your feet.
  gold +medium
  health -tiny
  say: {You pour, smile and stay out of the stories. The pay is fair, and the floor is very hard.|You keep the glasses full and your mouth shut. The merchant never learns your name, which suits you.|You pour for him and his rivals alike. Nobody notices you, and you go home with honest money and sore feet.}

@event company_magistrate
title: The Magistrate's Card Party
thread: company_night
class: routine
tone: tense
repeat: 3
art: cards
tags: money company_night
scene: A card table under a green lamp, a stern magistrate in a fur collar studying her hand, a student opposite her looking at the ceiling.
text: {A magistrate from the county court is holding a card party, and she likes to win, but not too easily.|The magistrate again. She deals you in without asking, and her eyes say she hasn't forgotten the last game.|The magistrate's party, and a new rule: whoever lets her win too obviously pays for the wine.} The landlady's look says: lose, gracefully.
> left: Lose exactly enough
  hint: Throw the game without being seen to.
  alignment -tiny
  challenge deceive adept-:
    win:
      gold +large
      mood -tiny
      say: {You lose by a hair after a brave fight. She is delighted, and tips accordingly. You feel faintly like a forger.|You let her catch your bluff in the last hand. She laughs for the first time all night, and pays you twice over.|You lose so well she thinks she beat you fairly. The money is good. Lying with cards is still lying.}
    lose:
      gold +tiny
      renown -small
      queue night_suspicion chance unlikely
      say: {She catches you discarding an ace and puts her cards down. "Don't patronise me." Word gets about, as word from a magistrate does.|You lose too obviously, and pay for the wine, and the story of it is round the village by noon.|She sees through you in three hands and says so to the whole table. The landlady doesn't look at you on the stair.}
> right: Deal and keep score
  hint: Honest work at the table, smaller pay.
  gold +medium
  mood -tiny
  say: {You deal, count and say nothing. It is the dullest money you've ever earned, and it's clean.|You keep the score, honestly, and she loses honestly, and doesn't tip the scorer.|You deal for four hours. The magistrate wins without help, and nods at you as if you'd had something to do with it.}

@event company_masked
title: The Masked Night
thread: company_night
class: routine
tone: eerie
repeat: 3
art: mask
tags: money company_night
scene: A candlelit upstairs room full of guests in feathered and lacquered masks, one masked figure watching a student without moving.
text: {Tonight every guest wears a mask, and nobody says why. The landlady hands you one too, plain black, no feathers.|The masks again. One guest, in lacquered silver, asks for you by the colour of your mask.|The masked night. The silver mask is here, and has saved you the seat beside it.} Without faces the room is easier to read, and harder.
> left: Sit with the masks
  hint: The best tips, and nobody's name.
  challenge negotiate adept- wild:
    win:
      gold +large
      guile +small
      mood -small
      say: {You learn to read a room by its hands. You go home with heavy pockets, no names, and a feeling you can't put down.|The silver mask asks you nothing at all, and tips like a duke. You lie awake wondering who that was.|Behind the masks the guests say what they mean. You come home rich and a little colder.}
    lose:
      gold +tiny
      mood -medium
      queue night_suspicion chance even
      say: {A masked guest won't take no for an answer, and nobody can see who it is. You leave by the back stair with a few coins and a very bad feeling.|The silver mask leans too close, and nobody else in the room seems to notice. You go home by the long way.|A guest takes your mask off, laughing, in front of everyone. Someone at the far table looks very like a prefect.}
> right: Work the door
  hint: Fair pay in the cold stairwell.
  gold +medium
  health -small
  say: {You take coats at the door all night. The stairwell is freezing, and every coat smells of a different perfume.|You keep the door, and turn away a guest the landlady points out. The draught gets into your bones.|You work the door and watch the masks arrive one by one. The silver one bows to you, which is worse than being spoken to.}

@event company_patron
title: A Guest Who Offers Patronage
thread: company_night
class: routine
tone: warm
repeat: 3
art: book
tags: money study company_night
scene: An elderly scholar in a moth-eaten velvet coat at a tavern table, a pile of rare books beside a purse, talking earnestly to a student.
text: {An old collector of books has asked for "the student who reads". He wants company and argument, and he says he funds promising young minds.|The old collector is back with a new pile of books and a new argument. He has been thinking about what you said, for a month.|The collector again. He calls you his protégé to the whole room now, which is flattering and a little alarming.}
> left: Take the patron's purse
  hint: Generous, and he'll want your evenings.
  gold +large
  study -small
  say: {He funds your books for the term, and wants you at his table every week. You argue till two, and sleep through a lecture.|The purse is generous and so is the argument, which runs until the candles do. Your coursework waits.|He pays you to disagree with him. You are good at it, and your exam reading is not getting done.}
> right: Ask to borrow his books
  hint: Learning instead of money.
  lore +medium
  gold +small
  say: {He beams, and lends you three books older than the castle. You read them on the walk home by moonlight.|He lends you his annotated copy, full of furious notes in the margin. They are better than the book.|"Finally," he says, "someone who wants the books more than the money." He gives you a small purse anyway.}

@event company_castle
title: Someone from the Castle
thread: company_night
class: routine
tone: tense
repeat: 3
art: eye
tags: money danger company_night
cast:
  guest: any where tagged(guest, teacher) and met(guest)
scene: A professor out of academic dress at a tavern card table, a glass of wine halfway to their mouth, looking up at a student holding a tray.
text: {Halfway up the stair you see {guest} at the corner table, out of robes, with a glass of wine and the air of someone who would rather not be seen either.|{guest} is at the corner table tonight. You both pretend this is a coincidence.|Tonight it is {guest} in the corner. Half the faculty seem to drink here, and there is an arrangement: nobody has seen anybody.}
> left: Serve {them} as if you'd never met
  hint: Discretion pays; a slip gets talked about.
  challenge deceive adept:
    win:
      gold +large
      rel guest +tiny
      say: {You serve {guest} like any guest, and {they} tip{s} like one, too generously. At breakfast {they} nod{s} to you as if nothing ever happened.|You keep a straight face all night. On the way out {guest} leaves a coin under {their} glass and, beside it, a very small nod.|Neither of you blinks. In class next week {guest} calls on you with something almost like respect.}
    lose:
      gold +small
      rel guest -small
      queue night_suspicion chance likely
      say: {You drop a glass when {guest} looks up. Everyone turns. {guest} leaves early, and is cold to you all week.|You say "Professor" before you can stop yourself. The table laughs. {guest} does not.|{guest} catches your eye and you both flinch, in front of everyone. By morning half the village knows who was where.}
> right: Slip back downstairs
  hint: Give up the night's pay to stay unseen.
  gold +tiny
  mood -small
  say: {You give the landlady an excuse and go down the back stair. It's a lost night's pay, and the walk home is sulky.|You hand your tray to another student and leave. {guest} never sees you; your purse notices.|You go home early, and lie awake doing the sum of what you didn't earn.}

@event company_wants_more
title: A Guest Who Wants More
thread: company_night
class: routine
tone: grim
repeat: 3
art: mask
tags: money danger company_night
scene: A well-dressed guest at a tavern table holding out a heavy purse, a student standing back from the table with arms folded, a landlady watching from the door.
text: {A guest with rings on every finger has been generous all night, and now pushes a heavy purse across the table. "Come back to my inn," he says. "Just to talk." The house rule is clear.|Another guest, another heavy purse, another invitation "just to talk". The landlady is busy across the room.|You know the look now: the generous hour, then the purse, then the invitation. Tonight it comes from a woman in pearls.}
> left: Call the landlady
  hint: The house rule; the tip goes with the guest.
  alignment +tiny
  gold +small
  mood -small
  say: {The landlady has the guest down the stair in a minute, purse and all. You're paid for the hours, and shaky for the rest of the night.|"The stair," says the landlady, and the guest takes it. You keep your wage and lose the tip, and your hands won't stop shaking.|The guest is shown out. The landlady pours you something strong and pays you for the night. It doesn't feel like enough.}
> right: Keep the purse, promise nothing
  hint: Talk them round and keep it; it can go wrong.
  alignment -tiny
  challenge deceive adept:
    win:
      gold +large
      mood -small
      say: {You let him believe tomorrow, and then the day after. He leaves the purse. You feel like a pickpocket with good manners.|You laugh, and keep the purse, and see the guest into a carriage, alone. It's the most you've earned in a night. You don't sleep.|You promise nothing in a way that sounds like something. She leaves happy and you leave rich, and a little ashamed.}
    lose:
      mood -medium
      health -tiny
      queue night_suspicion chance even
      say: {He sees through you, and takes his purse back with a hand that grips too hard. The landlady throws him out, too late.|The guest makes a scene on the stair, loudly calling you a cheat. A prefect on the stair hears every word.|She wants the purse back, loudly, and the landlady gives it to her. You go home with nothing but a bruise on your wrist.}

# ---------------- The high table, round after round (feedback 2026-10-20) ----------------
# A win with your purse at the high table doesn't end the night: Grisk offers the next hand at once (`queue … now`),
# each round dearer and harder than the last, three in all. Walking away is always open; a loss ends the run.

@event newt_high_table_raise
title: Let It Ride
trigger: queued
class: routine
tone: tense
repeat: 6
art: cards
tags: money danger
scene: A goblin banker in a velvet waistcoat pushing a tall stack of coins back into the middle of a green-lamped card table.
text: {Grisk sweeps your winnings into one neat stack and pushes it back into the middle of the table. "Let it ride," he says. "The second hand pays double. Most people stop here. Most people are dull."|Grisk is already shuffling. "Again?" he says, as if it were your idea. Your winnings sit in the middle of the table, waiting.|"Second hand," says Grisk, and the players at the next table stop pretending not to watch.} The new dealer is a woman who hasn't smiled once tonight.
> left: Let it ride
  hint: Your winnings on one hand; the table plays harder.
  alignment -tiny
  gold -large
  challenge deceive adept+ unlikely wild:
    win:
      gold +huge
      queue newt_high_table_last now
      say: {The unsmiling dealer turns her last card and looks at you properly for the first time. The pot is yours, and it's heavy.|You bluff on nothing and the whole table folds. Grisk laughs so hard he has to sit down.|You win the second hand by a single card. Somewhere behind you somebody says your name, which is not ideal.}
    lose:
      mood -small
      queue night_suspicion chance even
      say: {The dealer takes the hand without a flicker. Your winnings go back to the bank, and the walk home is very long.|You read her wrong, and she lets you know it. Grisk pats your shoulder. "That's the second hand," he says. "It's always the second hand."|The pot goes the other way. On the stair down, a lamp moves in the courtyard and you have to wait in the dark for it to pass.}
> right: Cash out
  hint: Walk away with what you won.
  mood +tiny
  say: {You stand, pocket your winnings and bow to the table. Grisk looks almost disappointed in you.|"Dull," says Grisk fondly, as you go. Your purse doesn't think so.|You leave while the leaving is good, and whistle all the way back to the castle, very quietly.}

@event newt_high_table_last
title: Everything on One Hand
trigger: queued
class: routine
tone: tense
repeat: 4
art: cards
tags: money danger
scene: A crowd of gamblers standing around one card table in a tavern back room, a goblin banker taking off his rings one by one.
text: {The room has gone quiet. Players from every other table have drifted over to stand around yours. Grisk takes off his rings, one by one, and sets them on the pot. "Everything," he says. "One hand. Then we see who goes home."|Grisk deals this one himself. Nobody has done that in years, someone whispers.|The whole back room is standing around your table now. Grisk rolls up his velvet sleeves.} Whatever is in the pot tonight is in it again, and more besides.
> left: Everything on one hand
  hint: The biggest pot in the room; Grisk deals.
  alignment -small
  gold -large
  challenge deceive expert- unlikely wild:
    win:
      gold +huge
      gold +medium
      renown +small
      say: {Grisk turns his last card, looks at yours, and starts to laugh. He puts his rings back on, one by one, and pushes the pot to you. "Don't come back for a month," he says. "I mean it kindly."|You win, and the back room applauds. By morning the village has three versions of the story, and in all of them you are taller.|The whole room breathes out at once. Grisk shakes your hand with both of his. "A real player," he says, and it sounds like a warning.}
    lose:
      mood -small
      queue night_suspicion chance likely
      say: {Grisk turns a card you didn't think was left in the deck. Your stake goes back to him, a fat slice of the night's winnings with it. He's very kind about it, which is worse.|You lose the hand on one card. The crowd drifts back to their own tables as if nothing happened. On the stair, a prefect's lamp is waiting.|The pot goes to Grisk. He counts it slowly, in front of you, and offers to call you a carriage. There are no carriages.}
> right: Leave on a winning streak
  hint: Two hands won, and a story to tell.
  renown +tiny
  mood +small
  say: {You stand up in the middle of the hush. "Another night," you say, and leave with two pots in your coat. Grisk applauds, alone, and then everyone does.|You refuse the last hand. The back room is torn between booing and admiration. Your purse settles it.|"Not tonight," you say. Grisk grins all his teeth. "That," he says, "is how you get to play again."}

# ---------------- Company nights that run late (feedback 2026-10-20) ----------------

@event company_late_hour
title: The Second Party
trigger: queued
class: routine
tone: tense
repeat: 8
art: cup
tags: money danger
scene: A landlady catching a tired student by the sleeve on a tavern stair long after midnight, a second party arriving noisily below.
text: {Past two, the landlady catches your sleeve on the stair. "Another party's just come up, and I'm a pair of hands short. Same pay again."|A second party is coming up the stair as yours goes down it. The landlady looks at you and raises an eyebrow.|"One more," says the landlady, "and I'll owe you." The castle will be waking in four hours.}
> left: Stay till dawn
  hint: Another party's pay; no sleep, and a later walk home.
  health -small
  queue any in company_night now
  queue night_suspicion chance unlikely
  say: {You take your jacket back off the peg. The second party is louder than the first.|You splash cold water on your face and go back up. The lamps are still lit, and so are the guests.|You stay. It will be light when you get home, and the castle's early risers will see you come in.}
> right: Go home to bed
  hint: One party is enough for one night.
  mood +tiny
  say: {You hand back the jacket and walk home under the last of the stars. Your bed has never been so welcome.|"Next time," you tell the landlady. You're asleep before the castle clock strikes three.|You leave the second party to someone else and sleep the four hours you have left like a stone.}

@event company_wedding
title: A Wedding Upstairs
thread: company_night
class: routine
tone: light
repeat: 3
art: music
tags: money company_night
scene: A noisy wedding party in a tavern's upstairs room, a fiddler on a table, a bride in a crooked flower crown dancing with her grandmother.
text: {A miller's daughter from the next valley married this afternoon, and the party has moved upstairs at the Newt to keep going.|Another wedding upstairs: a carter and a dressmaker, and both families determined to out-dance the other.|A wedding again, and the fiddler from last time, who waves at you with his bow.} The landlady wants the dancing kept going and the families kept apart.
> left: Keep everyone dancing
  hint: Dance with every aunt; big tips if the families mix.
  challenge perform journeyman:
    win:
      gold +large
      health -small
      say: {You dance with every aunt, two uncles and the bride. By midnight the families are singing the same song. Both fathers tip you.|You get the two grandmothers dancing together, and after that nobody can remember the feud. The tips are enormous; your feet are not.|The fiddler and you keep it going until the candles go out. You walk home with a pocket of coins and a flower in your hair.}
    lose:
      gold +small
      mood -small
      say: {You ask the wrong cousin to dance, and the families end up on opposite sides of the room. A thin tip, and a long night.|The groom's brother picks a fight with the bride's, and you're the one standing between them. The landlady pays you off early.|Nobody wants to dance with the hired help tonight. You stand by the wall for four hours.}
> right: Mind the food and the fire
  hint: Steady pay, no dancing.
  gold +medium
  health -tiny
  say: {You keep the platters full and the fire fed. Nobody notices you, which at a wedding is a kind of success.|You carry cake until your arms ache. The bride kisses your cheek on her way out, and you go home sticky and paid.|You mind the fire and watch the dancing. It's honest money, and the music is good.}

@event company_poets
title: The Poets' Supper
thread: company_night
class: routine
tone: light
repeat: 3
art: book
tags: money study company_night
scene: A long tavern table covered in wine bottles and loose manuscript pages, ink-stained poets arguing, one standing on a chair to recite.
text: {A society of village poets has taken the upstairs room for its supper, and needs someone to judge the contest.|{chosen(company_poets, left): The poets are back. Their president remembers your last verdict and has not forgiven it.|The poets are back. Their president remembers you pouring the wine, and asks whether you'd dare to judge this time.}|The poets' supper again, and tonight the contest is in rhyming couplets only, including the toasts.} The landlady says: whoever wins, the wine bill gets paid.
> left: Judge the contest
  hint: A clever verdict pleases everyone; a bad one pleases nobody.
  challenge appeal journeyman-:
    win:
      gold +large
      lore +tiny
      mood -tiny
      say: {You give a prize to everyone, each for something different, and each of them thinks theirs is the real one. The wine bill gets paid with a flourish.|Your verdict, in rhyming couplets, gets a standing ovation. The president pays you himself and asks for a copy.|You find something true to say about every poem, even the long one about turnips. They tip in verse and in coin.}
    lose:
      gold +tiny
      renown -tiny
      say: {You give the prize to the poem about the president's late wife. It was, it turns out, written by her rival. The supper ends early.|Your verdict starts an argument that becomes a fight that becomes a poem about you. It is not a kind poem.|Nobody agrees with you, loudly, in couplets.}
> right: Pour and listen
  hint: Plain pay, and a night of poems.
  gold +medium
  lore +tiny
  say: {You pour wine and listen to forty poems. Three of them are good, and you remember one for weeks.|You keep the glasses full and the poets talking. You go home with fair pay and a head full of rhymes.|You pour and say nothing, and learn more about metre than a term of lectures taught you.}

# ---------------- The boathouse ----------------

@event ring_on_yourself
title: Bet on Yourself
place: boathouse
slot: midnight
type: menu
class: routine
tone: tense
# The ring's other door (feedback 2026-10-16): the same chain as midnight_boathouse_ring, with a bookmaker.
repeat: 4
weight: 9
art: sword
tags: danger money
scene: A lantern-lit cellar under a boathouse, a scorched chalk ring on wet flagstones, a bookmaker holding up a slate of odds, a crowd in scarves.
on_show:
  unflag ring_last_standing ring_down
  chance unlikely:
    flag ring_last_standing
text: {The ring under the boathouse has a bookmaker now, with a slate. "Fancy yourself?" he says. "Put your money where your wand is. Cast what you like. Nobody down here tells."|The bookmaker knows your name and your odds, and they've shortened.|Your name is on the slate tonight before you've said you'll fight.} {flag(ring_last_standing): Across the top of his slate he has chalked LAST STANDING: step in tonight and you fight until you drop or have beaten four, or you pay the house a forfeit to walk out.|Win, and the next bout pays more.}
> left: Bet your purse on yourself
  alignment -tiny
  gold -medium
  challenge combat adept-:
    win:
      gold +large
      gold +medium
      renown +tiny
      health -small
      queue any in ring_cameo now chance unlikely
      queue ring_bout_2 now
      say: {You win, singed and grinning, and the bookmaker pays out through his teeth.|The crowd roars. You collect your bet and your opponent's grudging handshake.|Your name was on the slate before you agreed, and the crowd is bigger for it. You win, and the bookmaker pays you in front of all of them.}
    lose:
      unflag ring_last_standing
      health -medium
      queue night_suspicion chance even
      say: {You go down in the third exchange, and your purse goes with you. On the way home a light is on in the prefects' corridor.|A bad loss: the bet, your pride and both eyebrows.|The bigger crowd came to see you lose, it turns out. You oblige, and your purse goes with your pride.}
  if met(lyra):
    queue lyra_ring_word chance unlikely
> right: Fight for the purse only
  challenge combat adept-:
    win:
      gold +small
      health -small
      queue any in ring_cameo now chance unlikely
      queue ring_bout_2 now
      say: {A clean win for a small purse. The money-holder asks if you'll go again.|You win and bow to your opponent. The bookmaker is already chalking your name for the next bout.|You win the purse in front of the biggest crowd yet. The bookmaker sighs at the money he didn't make on you.}
    lose:
      unflag ring_last_standing
      health -medium
      say: {You lose, bow, and spend the walk home finding new bruises.|A loss, and the loser's purse, which is nothing at all.|You lose in front of the biggest crowd yet. At least it was only the purse.}
> no: Not tonight
  hint: Watch, and bet if you like.
  unflag ring_last_standing
  queue ring_side_bets now
  say: {The bookmaker shrugs and rubs your name off the slate.|"Your loss," says the bookmaker. "Or someone else's gain. Care to back one?"|You leave your name on the slate and your wand in your pocket.}

# Walking out of a last-standing night (the critic, 2026-10-28): every fight down here keeps a way out. The house keeps
# a forfeit from the purse and the crowd remembers it.
@preset ring_forfeit
desc: Leaving the ring on a last-standing night.
do:
  unflag ring_last_standing
  gold -medium
  renown -small
  mood -small

@event ring_bout_2
title: The Second Bout
trigger: queued
class: routine
tone: tense
art: sword
tags: danger money
scene: A chalk ring under a boathouse, a fresh opponent twirling a wand, a crowd pressing in with coins in their fists.
when: not flag(ring_down)
text: The money-holder points at the chalk again. {~A lanky girl steps in who quit the academy last spring. She kept the wand, and the duelling stance.|A fairground conjurer steps in, still in spangles, rolling a spark across his knuckles. The crowd knows him.|A hedge-hexer from past the village steps in, a cord of knotted charms at her wrist. Some of those knots aren't legal anywhere.} {flag(ring_last_standing): Last standing: stepping out now costs a forfeit.|The purse has doubled. Or you can take what you've won and go.}
> left: Fight on
  hint: A harder bout, a bigger purse.
  challenge combat adept:
    win:
      gold +large
      queue ring_bout_3 now
      say: {~She duels by the book she walked out on. You've read the later chapters. Her wand ends up in the crowd, and the purse ends up heavier.|The conjurer's sparks are all show. You aim at the hand that isn't waving, and he sits down in a shower of spangles.|You cut her charm-cord with your first spell. Whatever the knots were for, it doesn't happen, and she spits and yields.}
    lose:
      unflag ring_last_standing
      health -medium
      mood -small
      queue night_suspicion chance even
      say: {~She was the best in her year before she left, and she still is. She helps you up off the flagstones, which is worse.|He palms a spark, and while you watch it, the other hand ends the bout. You limp up the steps with the night's winnings and singed sleeves.|One knot comes undone, and something cold walks through your head. You come round outside, with your purse, and a headache that lasts a day.}
> right: {flag(ring_last_standing): Forfeit, and walk out|Take the purse and go}
  hint: {flag(ring_last_standing): Last standing: the house keeps a forfeit, and the crowd remembers.|Keep what you've won.}
  if not flag(ring_last_standing):
    mood +tiny
    say: {~You bow out with the purse in your fist. The dropout shrugs and finds someone else.|You leave the conjurer to the next fool, and count your winnings on the walk home.|The hexer laughs as you go, and ties another knot.}
  else:
    ring_forfeit
    say: You raise your hand and step out of the chalk. The bookmaker chalks FORFEIT across your name, takes the house's share of your purse in front of everyone, and the cellar hisses you up the stairs.

@event ring_bout_3
title: The Third Bout
trigger: queued
class: routine
tone: tense
art: sword
tags: danger money
scene: A packed boathouse cellar roaring at a chalk ring, a wizard in a long coat stepping in, lantern light swinging.
when: not flag(ring_down)
text: Two down. The cellar is packed now and the betting is loud. {~The house sends in a Conclave clerk in ink-stained cuffs, moonlighting for the purse. He reads your stance like a form.|The house sends in a prefect who lost the sash last year, and has been angry about it ever since.|The house sends in someone in a hood whose wand hand is grey to the wrist. The steps go quiet.} {flag(ring_last_standing): Last standing: the chalk, or the forfeit.|One more win and the purse is the biggest yet. Or walk away a winner.}
> left: Fight on
  hint: Harder still, and worth it.
  challenge combat adept+:
    win:
      gold +large
      gold +small
      renown +small
      queue ring_bout_4 now
      say: {~He foresees every spell you planned. So you stop planning. The clerk goes down muttering about irregular procedure, and the cellar shakes.|The ex-prefect fights like a rulebook with a grudge. You break a rule he didn't know, and the purse is fat.|The hood falls back as they go down: nobody you know, and a face older than the hands. The crowd chants your name to drown the quiet.}
    lose:
      unflag ring_last_standing
      health -large
      queue night_suspicion chance even
      say: {~The clerk dismantles you, politely, by the numbers. You wake on the steps with the purse still in your pocket and a ringing ear.|The ex-prefect's ward doesn't move, and neither, after a while, do you. Your ribs remember it all week.|The hooded caster drinks something out of you that isn't blood. You go down and stay down, and someone carries you to the door.}
> right: {flag(ring_last_standing): Forfeit, and walk out|Take the purse and go}
  hint: {flag(ring_last_standing): Last standing: the house keeps a forfeit, and the crowd remembers.|Leave a winner.}
  if not flag(ring_last_standing):
    mood +small
    say: {~You leave the clerk waiting in the chalk with his forms. The crowd boos, and your purse doesn't care.|The ex-prefect calls you a coward. Nobody else does; you're holding too much money.|You bow to the hood and climb the steps. Some fights are better not known.}
  else:
    ring_forfeit
    say: Two down, and you step out. It's a forfeit under the rules: the house takes its cut of your winnings, and the crowd, which had money on you, lets you know about it all the way to the door.

@event ring_bout_4
title: The Last Bout
trigger: queued
class: routine
tone: tense
art: trophy
tags: danger money
scene: A boathouse cellar gone silent around a chalk ring, the night's champion stepping in, wand raised, every lantern turned toward the fighters.
when: not flag(ring_down)
text: Three down, and the cellar has gone quiet. {~The ring's own champion steps in: a duellist the academy expelled, who hasn't lost down here in a year.|The bookmaker himself takes off his coat. His wand was in it all along, and the old hands step back from the chalk.|A girl in a borrowed cloak steps in, a runaway from Thornwick. Her shadow moves a moment after she does, and the old hands start betting against you.} {flag(ring_last_standing): Last standing: win this and the night is yours.|Win, and the cellar will talk about you for a term. Or go while you can still walk.}
> left: Fight the last bout
  hint: Expert opposition; the night's whole purse.
  challenge combat expert-:
    win:
      gold +huge
      renown +small
      health -small
      if flag(ring_last_standing):
        gold +large
        renown +small
      unflag ring_last_standing
      say: {~The champion's ward breaks at last, and the cellar stamps until dust comes down from the boathouse floor. You go home with a purse you can barely lift.|He reads your next spell out of your head every time, so you cast the one you hadn't thought of. He goes down laughing and pays you from his own coat.|You hit the shadow, not the girl, and she yields, grinning, and buys you a drink you can't hold. You walk home sore and rich and famous in one cellar.}
    lose:
      unflag ring_last_standing
      health -large
      queue night_suspicion chance likely
      say: {~The champion ends it in one spell, and you understand why the academy wanted them gone. You wake on the steps with your night's winnings and a long walk.|The bookmaker casts things the library keeps chained, and wins clean. You crawl up the steps holding your purse and your ribs.|You can't tell the girl from her shadow, and the wrong one hits you. The crowd is so loud the prefects must hear it.}
> right: {flag(ring_last_standing): Forfeit, and walk out|Take the purse and go}
  hint: {flag(ring_last_standing): Last standing: the house keeps a forfeit, and the crowd remembers.|Three wins is a fine night.}
  if not flag(ring_last_standing):
    mood +small
    say: {~You bow out with three wins and a heavy purse. The champion nods: next time.|You don't fight the man who sets the odds, and knows your thoughts. You take your winnings before he can change his mind.|You look at the runaway's shadow and decide three is plenty.}
  else:
    ring_forfeit
    say: You step out before the last bout. The bookmaker counts out the forfeit from your purse with great ceremony, and the cellar boos you, and a few people, quietly, look relieved for you.

@event ring_side_bets
title: Side Bets
trigger: queued
type: menu
class: routine
tone: tense
art: dice
tags: money danger
scene: Students on the steps of a boathouse cellar passing coins to a bookmaker while two fighters circle in a chalk ring below.
text: {You find a place on the cellar steps. The bookmaker works his way along them, slate in hand.|From the steps you can see the fighters' wand hands, which tell you more than their faces. The bookmaker is coming round.|You know the regulars on the steps now, and who bets with their heart. The bookmaker holds out his slate.}
> left: Back the favourite
  hint: A small stake on the likely winner.
  gold -small
  challenge research using guile journeyman luck:
    win:
      gold +medium
      say: {Your fighter wins. You collect quietly and leave before anyone remembers your face.|You read the wand hands right. The bookmaker pays you as if it hurt.|The favourite wins, as favourites do. You pocket a small, sensible profit.}
    lose:
      say: {Your fighter goes down in the first exchange. So does your stake.|You picked the wand hand, not the wizard. The bookmaker smiles.|The favourite slips on the wet stone, and your crowns slip with them.}
> under: Back the underdog
  hint: A bigger stake against the odds.
  gold -medium
  challenge research using guile adept unlikely luck:
    win:
      gold +large
      gold +medium
      say: {The underdog wins, and you're the only one on the steps who backed them. The bookmaker counts it out very slowly.|Your long shot lands one spell, and it's enough. The steps turn round to look at you.|You saw what nobody else did, the limp in the favourite's step. You walk home heavy with silver.}
    lose:
      say: {The underdog is an underdog for a reason. Your stake is gone in a minute.|A brave fight, a long fall, and your crowns go with it.|You backed your heart. Your purse pays for it.}
> home: Go home
  say: {You watch one bout and go home with your purse unopened.|You leave before the bookmaker reaches you.|You climb the steps into the cold and leave the ring to its noise.}

# A character who would fight in an illegal ring steps into the chalk out of turn (feedback 2026-10-25): drawn by chance
# after a won first or second bout (`queue any in ring_cameo now chance unlikely`), before the next one, never the last.
# Only those whose cast fits a cellar ring have a card here: reckless Corvin, proud Lyra, Bram out of his sash, fierce
# Ilse. Losing (or walking out) ends the night: the next bout's `when` reads ring_down.
@pool ring_cameo
desc: Someone you know steps into the boathouse ring out of turn, between bouts.
trigger: queued
class: routine
tone: tense
repeat: 12
art: duel
tags: danger money

@event ring_cameo_corvin
title: A Familiar Grin
pool: ring_cameo
actor: corvin
when: met(corvin) and around(corvin)
scene: A grinning young man vaulting a rope into a chalk ring in a lantern-lit cellar, sleeves rolled, a wand spinning in his fingers.
text: Before the next bout someone vaults the rope into the chalk: Corvin, sleeves rolled, wand spinning. He sees you and his grin slips. "Saint. Of course it's you." Then, low: "I owe three people money, and one of them has started describing what happens next. The house pays double for a grudge match. So hate me. Loudly."
> left: Fight him for real
  hint: He fights dirty, and fast.
  challenge combat vs corvin.guile:
    win:
      gold +medium
      renown +tiny
      rel corvin -tiny
      say: You knock his wand into the crowd on the fifth exchange. "Rude," he says from the flagstones, and laughs, but his eyes go to the bookmaker's slate and stay there a moment too long.
    lose:
      flag ring_down
      unflag ring_last_standing
      health -small
      rel corvin +tiny
      say: He was never where your spell landed. He hauls you up by the collar, collects his purse, and presses a few coins of it into your hand. "Your cut, for the hating. You were very convincing."
> right: Put on a show, and let him win
  hint: {flag(ring_last_standing): Last standing: this counts as a forfeit.|Your night ends; his debt doesn't.}
  flag ring_down
  alignment -tiny
  rel corvin +medium
  say: You shout, he shouts, sparks fly everywhere but at each other, and you go down with a groan the back row will talk about all week. The bettors who backed you never know. Corvin does. "I owe you," he says on the steps. "Add it to the list."
  if flag(ring_last_standing):
    ring_forfeit
    say: On a last-standing night it counts as a forfeit: the bookmaker takes the house's share of your winnings on the way out.

@event ring_cameo_lyra
title: Someone Worth Losing To
pool: ring_cameo
actor: lyra
when: met(lyra) and around(lyra)
scene: A proud red-haired student in a chalk ring, gloves off, hair tied back, a ribbon of flame curling from her wand, a cellar crowd roaring.
text: The crowd parts. Lyra Vaneshade is in the chalk, gloves off, hair tied back. "So it's true." Her voice carries over the whole cellar. "You're throwing your talent away down here, in front of drunks. Fine. Then you'll lose it to someone worth losing to." Somebody is already shouting odds on House Vaneshade.
> left: Fight her
  hint: In front of everyone.
  challenge combat vs lyra.arcana:
    win:
      gold +medium
      renown +small
      rel lyra +small
      say: Her flame breaks on your last spell and she goes down on one knee in the chalk, in front of all of them. She stands, white to the lips. "Again," she says. "In the hall. Where it counts." The crowd will be talking about it for a term.
    lose:
      flag ring_down
      unflag ring_last_standing
      health -small
      rel lyra +tiny
      say: She takes you apart in four exchanges, beautifully. Then she walks you up the steps herself, holding your elbow. "Now you know," she says, "where you belong. It isn't here."
> right: Walk out with her
  hint: {flag(ring_last_standing): Last standing: this counts as a forfeit.|The crowd won't like it.}
  flag ring_down
  renown -tiny
  rel lyra +medium
  say: You step out of the chalk and offer her your arm. The cellar boos you both up the steps. Outside, in the cold, Lyra lets go of your arm and doesn't say thank you. She does walk you all the way back.
  if flag(ring_last_standing):
    ring_forfeit
    say: On a last-standing night it counts as a forfeit: the bookmaker takes the house's share of your winnings on the way out.

@event ring_cameo_bram
title: Out of Uniform
pool: ring_cameo
actor: bram
when: met(bram) and around(bram)
scene: A broad young man pulling back a hood in a chalk ring, no prefect's sash, wand gripped tight, staring at someone he didn't expect.
text: The next challenger steps in hooded, and drops the hood: Bram Hollis, without his red sash. He sees you and goes the colour of it. "You didn't see me," he says, fast and low. "I didn't see you. Nobody down here writes anything down. That's the point." Then, loud, for the crowd: "Well? Get in the chalk!"
> left: Fight him
  hint: He hits like a wall.
  challenge combat vs bram.vigor:
    win:
      gold +medium
      renown +tiny
      rel bram +small
      say: His ward finally gives, and he sits down hard in the chalk. Then something odd happens: he laughs, a real one, out loud. "Nobody's ever beaten me where my father couldn't hear about it," he says. He offers you his hand.
    lose:
      flag ring_down
      unflag ring_last_standing
      health -small
      rel bram +tiny
      say: He walks through your spells like rain and puts you on the flagstones. On the steps he stops. "Not a word," he says. "And not a demerit either. We were never here."
> right: Let him have the bout
  hint: {flag(ring_last_standing): Last standing: this counts as a forfeit.|Leave him his secret, and the purse.}
  flag ring_down
  rel bram +medium
  say: You step back out of the chalk and bow to him. He stares at you as if it were a trick. On the stairs to the dormitory next morning, your name isn't in his ledger, and he looks away first.
  if flag(ring_last_standing):
    ring_forfeit
    say: On a last-standing night it counts as a forfeit: the bookmaker takes the house's share of your winnings on the way out.

@event ring_cameo_ilse
title: Frost in the Chalk
pool: ring_cameo
actor: ilse
when: met(ilse) and around(ilse)
scene: A girl in white furs in a chalk ring, frost creeping from her boots across the flagstones, lantern flames shrinking, a crowd stepping back.
text: The cellar goes cold. The lanterns shrink. A girl in white furs steps into the chalk and frost creeps out from her boots across the flagstones: Ilse Dumont. "They said the academy's best come down here," she says. She looks you over. "They lied. But you'll do."
> left: Fight her
  hint: She was raised to fight.
  challenge combat vs ilse.arcana:
    win:
      gold +medium
      renown +small
      rel ilse +small
      say: She slips on her own ice and your spell catches her before she's up. She stays down a moment, then laughs, a short Northern sound. "Again," she says. "Not tonight. But again." She takes her purse and leaves the cellar colder.
    lose:
      flag ring_down
      unflag ring_last_standing
      health -small
      rel ilse +tiny
      say: Rime closes over your wand hand and you can't feel it to cast. She stands over you, unimpressed. "You fight like someone taught you," she says. "Find someone who didn't." Your fingers ache till morning.
> right: Ask her why she's down here
  hint: {flag(ring_last_standing): Last standing: this counts as a forfeit.|Talk, and give up the night's next bout.}
  flag ring_down
  rel ilse +small
  lore +tiny
  say: She doesn't answer. Instead she tells you your stance is wrong, then fixes it, roughly, with her boot. "Southerners," she says, as if that explained everything. The crowd boos you both, and she doesn't seem to hear.
  if flag(ring_last_standing):
    ring_forfeit
    say: On a last-standing night it counts as a forfeit: the bookmaker takes the house's share of your winnings on the way out.

@event lyra_ring_word
title: Lyra Has Heard
trigger: queued
defer: off_campus or not around(lyra)
class: incident
tone: tense
actor: lyra
art: sword
tags: social
scene: A proud red-haired student blocking a corridor with folded arms, a duelling glove tucked in her belt.
text: Lyra blocks the corridor. "The ring under the boathouse." She says it as if it were a smell. "Duelling for coins in a cellar, in front of drunks, with spells nobody will report. You can actually duel. I've seen you. This is beneath you." A pause. "I'd hate to lose to someone who does it."
> left: "Then spar with me instead."
  rel lyra +small
  health -tiny
  say: She stares, then almost smiles. "Tomorrow, the Duelling Hall, six sharp." She beats you, and then shows you why, which from Lyra is close to an apology for the corridor.
> right: "It pays. You wouldn't know."
  rel lyra -medium
  gold +tiny
  say: Her face shuts like a door. "No," she says. "I wouldn't." She walks off. Later the Newt's landlady hands back the deposit you left on your bar tab: Lyra has paid it off. That is somehow worse.

@event grisk_pickup
title: A Parcel at the Boathouse
place: boathouse
slot: midnight
class: routine
tone: tense
repeat: 5
weight: 12
actor: grisk
when: flag(grisk_contact) and not flag(grisk_parcel) and not flag(grisk_gone)
art: pouch
tags: money danger
scene: A dark boathouse jetty at midnight, a small goblin holding out a parcel wrapped in oilcloth, the lake black behind him.
text: {Grisk is waiting on the jetty, a parcel in oilcloth under his arm. "Into the village by tomorrow afternoon. A cobbler's back door. Don't open it, don't drop it, don't be seen."|The jetty, the oilcloth, Grisk's teeth in the dark. "Same arrangement," he says.|Grisk again, and a heavier parcel than last time.} {It ticks faintly.|It's cold enough to frost your gloves.|It smells of oranges and something under the oranges.}
> left: Take the parcel
  meet grisk
  alignment -tiny
  flag grisk_parcel
  queue grisk_delivery in 1
  say: {You take it. It is heavier than it looks, and the walk back to your room is very long.|You hide it under your bed. You don't sleep well with it there.|You take the heavier parcel. It smells of oranges, and under the oranges of something you decide not to name.}
> right: Not this month
  meet grisk
  mood -tiny
  say: {"Next month, then," says Grisk, as if it were already arranged. He tucks the parcel away. You feel watched all the way back up the lawn.|Grisk shrugs. "There's always someone." You don't like how true it sounds.|You turn down the heavier parcel. Grisk weighs it in his hand, then in his mind. "Your loss," he says. "Or not."}

@event grisk_delivery
title: The Cobbler's Back Door
trigger: queued
slot: afternoon
defer: off_campus
class: incident
tone: tense
art: pouch
tags: money danger
scene: A narrow village lane in daylight, a student with an oilcloth parcel under one arm walking past a prefect who is buying apples.
text: {~You carry Grisk's parcel down to the village in broad daylight.|Grisk's parcel, the village lane, and too many people about.|The parcel is in your satchel and the satchel is suddenly the heaviest thing you own.} Bram is at the apple stall. The cobbler's back door is past him.
> left: Walk past as if you belong
  unflag grisk_parcel
  challenge deceive vs bram.guile +1:
    win:
      gold +large
      alignment -tiny
      say: {~You nod to Bram, buy an apple, and knock twice at the cobbler's door. A hand takes the parcel; another gives you a purse. Your heart takes an hour to slow down.|The door opens before you knock. Coins, a nod, done. You eat the apple on the way home and taste none of it.|The satchel feels heavier with every step past Bram. He waves. You wave. The cobbler pays, and your hands shake until supper.}
    lose:
      gain watched
      flag grisk_angry
      mood -small
      queue grisk_cross in 1
      say: {~Bram calls your name. You drop the parcel in a horse trough and walk on, very calmly. Bram follows you halfway home.|You panic at the corner and leave the parcel behind a water butt. When you go back, it's gone, and Bram is writing in his notebook.|Bram asks to see inside your satchel, kindly. You show him your books, and he never looks in the other pocket. You walk home sick.}
> right: Leave it at the door and run
  unflag grisk_parcel
  gold +small
  say: {~You go the long way round, drop it on the cobbler's step and run. The cobbler pays you, short, for the lack of manners.|You don't knock, and you don't wait. Short pay, slipped under your door that night.|You drop the satchel's weight on the cobbler's step and run. He pays you short, and you're glad to be rid of it.}

@event grisk_cross
title: Grisk Is Not Pleased
trigger: queued
class: incident
tone: grim
actor: grisk
art: chain
tags: money danger
scene: A goblin in a velvet waistcoat sitting on a student's bed uninvited, a ledger open on his knee, the dormitory window open behind him.
text: Grisk is sitting on your bed. You didn't leave the window open. "A parcel was lost," he says pleasantly, turning a page in his ledger. "Lost things have a price. I've written it down. Would you like to see?"
> left: Put it on your account
  meet grisk
  unflag grisk_angry
  if has(in_debt):
    debt_paid = debt_paid - 2
    mood -small
    say: "Two more months," he says, and writes it neatly. "Such a good customer." He leaves by the window. You don't sleep with it open again.
  else:
    gain in_debt
    debt_paid = 0
    debt_missed = 0
    mood -small
    say: He writes your name in the ledger under a new line: one parcel, six payments. "Twelve a month," he says, "as usual." He leaves by the window. You don't sleep with it open again.
> right: Tell him you're done
  meet grisk
  unflag grisk_angry grisk_contact
  flag grisk_gone
  health -small
  renown -tiny
  say: "Everyone is, eventually," Grisk says. On your way to breakfast, the stair you always take collapses three steps under you. Nobody saw. Everybody heard. You never see him again.

# ---------------- The refectory kitchens ----------------

@event kitchen_stores
title: The Stores
place: refectory
slot: midnight
class: routine
tone: tense
repeat: 4
weight: 9
art: meal
tags: money danger
scene: A dark storeroom behind a castle kitchen, sacks of sugar and crates of oranges stacked high, a student with an empty satchel.
text: {Behind the kitchens is the storeroom, full of sugar, spices and oranges from the south. The village pays well for oranges in winter, and nobody counts the crates.|The storeroom again. You know which crate is at the back and never counted.|The stores have a new padlock, which is mostly decorative.} {A mouse watches you without judgement.|The kitchen cat does judge you.|Somewhere a pot is still dripping.}
> left: Fill a satchel to sell
  alignment -small
  gold +small
  queue night_suspicion chance even
  say: {Oranges, a cone of sugar, a jar of saffron. The village grocer asks no questions and pays in coppers. Somebody in the castle will be short of saffron.|A full satchel and an empty conscience, or nearly. On the way out, the scullery door bangs behind you.|The new padlock opens with a hairpin. You fill the satchel, and the grocer pays, and you wonder who'll be blamed for the lock.}
> right: Take one orange and go
  mood +tiny
  say: {You eat it in the dark by the banked fire. It's the best orange you have ever eaten, and you know exactly why.|One orange. You leave the peel in the fire so nobody finds it.|You take one orange past the new padlock and feel worse about it than about all the crates.}

@event kitchen_night_baker
title: The Night Baker
place: refectory
slot: midnight
class: routine
tone: warm
repeat: 4
weight: 9
art: meal
tags: nature
scene: A warm castle bakehouse at midnight, a flour-dusted old baker kneading dough by the light of an open oven, a student leaning in the doorway.
text: {The bakehouse behind the kitchens is lit. The night baker, flour to the elbows, looks up. "Hungry or helpful?" she says. "Both is allowed."|The night baker waves you in without looking up. "Dough's waiting."|The bakehouse smells of tomorrow's bread. The baker has saved you a heel of yesterday's.} {The oven sighs.|A tray of rolls is cooling by the door.|She is humming something old.}
> left: Knead until the oven's hot
  lose hungry
  health -tiny
  mood +small
  say: {You knead until your arms shake. She gives you the first roll out of the oven and no lecture at all.|An hour of flour and warmth, and a hot roll for your trouble. You go back to bed with your hands smelling of bread, and sleep like a stone.|You knead while she hums something old. When the loaves go in, she toasts yesterday's heel for you, and you eat it on the step.}
> right: Just sit by the oven
  lose hungry
  mood +tiny
  say: {You sit by the open oven with your knees pulled up. She works, talks to the dough, and lets you be.|You sit by the oven with a roll from the cooling tray while she works. She doesn't ask why you're up. You don't ask why she is.|You eat the heel of bread and warm your feet. Upstairs, the castle is cold; down here, it never is.}

# ---------------- The Restricted Section ----------------

@event restricted_page_buyer
title: A Buyer for Old Pages
place: restricted_section
slot: midnight
class: routine
tone: eerie
repeat: 3
weight: 8
when: met(grisk)
art: book
tags: money danger mystery
scene: A dim Restricted Section aisle at midnight, a student holding a razor over an illuminated page, a black book watching from the shelf above.
text: {Grisk mentioned, once, that a collector pays for illuminated pages "of a certain age". The Restricted Section is full of them.|{flag(sold_page): There's a gap on the shelf where the last page you sold came from.|The illuminated book you read last time is back on its shelf, untouched.}|The illuminated books lean away from you as you pass.} {A gold-leaf dragon glints on one page.|One margin is full of tiny stars, all black.|A page here is worth a month's board.}
> left: Cut a page
  flag sold_page
  alignment -small
  gold +medium
  queue night_suspicion chance even
  say: {One clean cut. The book doesn't cry out, which is somehow worse. The collector pays well, and never asks where.|You sell the page and keep the guilt, which no one will buy.|The books lean away, and you cut a page anyway. A month's board, in one stroke. You don't come back to this aisle for a long time.}
> right: Read it instead
  lore +small
  mana -tiny
  say: {You read the illuminated page by moonlight. It's a recipe for honey cakes, in exquisite gold leaf, and the gilding draws at your mana the whole time you read. You leave it where it is.|The page is a list of stars, by name, and each name pulls a little magic out of you as you read it. You learn three, and put the book back.|The books lean away from you, then back, as you read instead of cut. The page is a map of the castle as it was. It costs you a little magic to look at it.}

# ---------------- The observatory roof ----------------

@event midnight_roof_partner
title: The Roof, Together
place: observatory
slot: midnight
class: routine
tone: warm
repeat: 4
weight: 10
cast:
  partner: any where bond(partner) == "partner" and around(partner)
art: star
tags: romance nature
scene: Two students lying side by side on an observatory roof at midnight under a sky full of stars, one blanket between them.
text: {You've brought {partner} up to the observatory roof, with a stolen blanket and two cups of cocoa going cold.|{partner} knows the way up now, and goes first.|{partner} has brought the blanket this time.} {The stars are very close.|A meteor, then another.|The castle below is all asleep.}
> left: Stay until the sky turns
  spark partner +small
  health -small
  queue night_suspicion chance unlikely
  say: {You name the stars between you, wrong, and argue about it, and stay until the sky goes grey. The stair down is lit by a prefect's lamp you just avoid.|You fall asleep up there and wake to the first bell, frozen and happy.|{partner} wraps you both in the blanket {they} brought and talks you to sleep. The castle wakes up around you.}
> right: An hour, then back
  rel partner +small
  mood +tiny
  say: {One hour, one blanket, back in bed before anyone stirs. You can still feel where {partner} was leaning on you.|You go down early. {partner} squeezes your hand on the stair, which is better than any star.|One hour under {partner}'s blanket. {They} fold{s} it and give{s} it to you at the bottom of the stair: "For next time."}

@event kael_midnight_roof
title: Kael at Midnight
place: observatory
actor: kael
slot: midnight
class: routine
tone: warm
repeat: 4
weight: 12
art: snow
tags: social
scene: A northern student sitting on the observatory parapet at midnight with frost spreading from his hands, the lake far below, the sky thick with stars.
text: {Kael is on the observatory parapet, which he shouldn't be, with frost spreading from his fingers into the stone. He moves over to make room.|Kael again, on the parapet, the night's cold not touching him.|Kael has left room on the parapet before you arrive.} {"I can't sleep south of the mountains," he says. "Too warm."|He is watching the lake, not the stars.|"Northern stars," he says, pointing at nothing you can see.}
> left: Sit with him
  meet kael
  rel kael +small
  health -tiny
  say: {You sit until your fingers ache from the cold. He names northern constellations in a language that sounds like snow falling. It's the most he's ever said.|He doesn't talk. Neither do you. When you go, he says "Goodnight," as if it were difficult, and it was.|He's left room, so you take it. He points out a northern star you can't see, and you pretend you can. He knows, and is pleased.}
> right: Ask what he's watching for
  meet kael
  lore +tiny
  rel kael -tiny
  say: {"For it to wake," he says, and then shuts up like a book. You've pressed too hard. But you know which way he faces now.|"Nothing," he says, too fast. He's watching the middle of the lake. You leave him to it, colder than when you came.|"Home," he says, about the northern stars, and nothing more. It's the most honest thing he's told you, and he looks away after.}

# ---------------- The gargoyle passage ----------------

@event corvin_note_was_mine
title: Second Timetable
place: gargoyle_passage
actor: corvin
slot: midnight
class: milestone
tone: light
when: seen(night_lead_in) > 0 and rel(corvin) >= 6
art: map
tags: social mystery
scene: A secret passage behind a gargoyle at midnight, a grinning dark-haired student holding up a thumbnail-sized silver map, chalk marks on the wall behind him.
text: In the gargoyle passage, Corvin holds up a map no bigger than a thumbnail. "Recognise it? The castle has a second timetable. It starts at midnight." He grins. "Who did you think writes notes like that? Prefects?"
> left: "Why me?"
  meet corvin
  rel corvin +medium
  mood -tiny
  say: "Because you looked at that door at the back of the library the way I do." He shrugs, and doesn't joke. "And because it's lonely, knowing all the doors." You don't sleep much, after.
> right: "You could have signed it."
  meet corvin
  rel corvin +small
  guile +tiny
  say: "And spoil it?" He tucks the map into your pocket. "Keep it. The doors move. The map knows." You check it in the morning, and one of the six doors has moved.

@event corvin_midnight_watch
title: Corvin Keeps Watch
place: gargoyle_passage
actor: corvin
slot: midnight
class: routine
tone: light
repeat: 4
weight: 10
art: lantern
tags: social
scene: A dark corridor at midnight, a dark-haired student leaning out of a niche to wave someone past, a prefect's lantern turning a far corner.
text: {Corvin leans out of a niche as you pass. "Bram's on the east stair. Take the west. You owe me nothing, I'm a saint."|Corvin's in his niche again with an apple and a list of where every prefect is.|"West stair," says Corvin, before you ask.} {He looks tired.|He has a new scratch on his cheek.|He's whistling, which he shouldn't.}
> left: Keep him company
  meet corvin
  rel corvin +small
  health -tiny
  say: {You share his apple and his niche. He tells you which tapestry hides a cupboard full of confiscated wands. You're up till the bells.|You keep watch with him for an hour. He's funnier at midnight, and sadder, and pretends to be neither.|He whistles until you shush him, then whistles quieter. You keep watch till the bell and learn three new routes.}
> right: Take the west stair
  meet corvin
  guile +tiny
  say: {The west stair is dark and empty, as promised. Corvin's map is never wrong. You wonder who taught him to be so careful.|You slip past Bram's lamp with a corridor to spare.|West stair, before you've asked. Corvin's whistling follows you down it, far too loud, and nobody catches either of you.}

# ---------------- The lakeshore ----------------

@event midnight_swim
title: The Lake at Midnight
pool: lakeshore_routine
slot: midnight
tone: warm
art: wave
tags: nature
scene: A moonlit lakeshore at midnight, clothes folded on a rock, a swimmer's head and a ring of ripples in the silver water.
text: {season == "winter": The lake at midnight has a skin of ice at the edges and stars in the middle.|{~The lake at midnight is black and silver and perfectly still.|Nobody swims after curfew, which is why you do.|The water is warm from the day and the air is not.}} {clock(hollowstar) >= 5: Far out, a ring of frost forms and fades.|{~An owl crosses the moon.|Somewhere on the far shore, a lantern.|A fish jumps, very loudly.}}
> left: Swim out to the middle
  mood +medium
  health -small
  say: {~You swim until the castle is a toy and the stars are all around you. The cold gets into your bones and stays till breakfast.|You float on your back in the middle of the lake. Something far below hums, once. You swim back faster than you swam out.|You swim out and turn on your back. The stars and the water are the same colour, and you lose track of which is which. You swim back slowly.}
> right: Paddle, then get dry
  mood +small
  health -tiny
  say: {~Knee-deep, then waist-deep, then out, gasping and laughing at yourself. Your teeth chatter all the way up the lawn.|A quick, cold paddle, and back to bed glowing.|You paddle until the fish jumps right beside you. You're out of the water before you've decided to be.}

@event grey_on_the_shore
title: Someone Else Who Can't Sleep
place: lakeshore
slot: midnight
class: story
tone: eerie
when: clock(hollowstar) >= 2 and seen(hs_grey_stranger) > 0 and not flag(grey_shore_seen)
weight: 14
art: mask
tags: mystery hollowstar danger
thread: hollowstar
scene: A moonlit lakeshore at midnight, a tall man in a grey coat sitting on an upturned boat, frost spreading from his boots across the pebbles.
text: The man in grey is sitting on an upturned boat at the water's edge, as if he'd been waiting for a ferry. "You can't sleep either," he says pleasantly. "Nobody sleeps well near this lake. Sit. I won't keep you long. I only want to tell you what the headmistress won't."
> left: Sit and listen
  flag grey_shore_seen
  alignment -tiny
  lore +small
  mood -small
  say: He tells you, kindly, that the founders were frightened people who buried what they couldn't understand, and that it has been lonely down there for three hundred years. It sounds true. You walk back with it, and can't put it down.
> right: Walk away
  flag grey_shore_seen
  alignment +tiny
  health -tiny
  say: You turn your back and walk, and the cold follows you all the way up the lawn like a dog. At the door you look back. The boat is empty, and frosted white.
